<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Issa (clan)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Issa Somali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Issa are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan of the Dir family, living in Djibouti, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. In the 1880s French officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonce-lagarde">Léonce Lagarde</a> signed protection treaties with Issa clans, and their coast became part of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. Rivalry between the Issa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> shaped the colony's politics, and Djibouti's first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a>, was an Issa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/issa-clan.jpg" alt="Issa (clan)" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skilla1st · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Issa_Tomb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Issa, also spelled Esa or Aysa and in Somali Ciise, are a northern Somali clan of the Dir clan family. They have close lineal ties with other Dir groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabuursi</a>, the Surre, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaal">Biimaal</a>, the Gurgura and the Bursuk, and wider ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawiye">Hawiye</a> and other clans that trace their descent to the common ancestor Samaale. Issa elders tell that several centuries ago their forefathers migrated from Zayla and gathered on Sitti Mountain, in what is now Aisha Wereda. Sheikh Issa's tomb probably dates from before the local arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-19th century the Issa were the most important group on the trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> to the coastal towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. They protected merchants and their goods in return for taxes, which helped formalise the role of the abban, and they held a near monopoly on raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and leading convoys. On 14 November 1875 Issa warriors attacked an Egyptian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-munzinger">Werner Munzinger</a> marching inland from Tadjoura, killing Munzinger, his wife and most of his command, which ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s ambition to expand into Abyssinia.</p><p>During the Italian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935/36 the Issa fought on the Italian side. On 13 August 1960 Ethiopian troops killed Issa men in the Aysha massacre, and in 1971 and 1972 the Ethiopian army killed hundreds of Issa and seized nearly 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. In 1987 the autonomous region of Dire Dawa was created for them, and after 1991 their areas in Ethiopia became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Issa live by a customary law, the Xeer Ciise, made by their elders and built on the sharing of power among the twelve Issa sub-clans. Forty-four elders met in month-long conferences on Mount Sitti, each sub-clan feeding them in turn, and drew up 362 unwritten laws known as hera. According to tradition a cloud once hung over the representatives of the Wardiq sub-clan, a sign taken as divine, and the Issa leader, the Ugaas, has been chosen from the Wardiq ever since. The Issa make up more than half the population of Djibouti.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Issa (clan)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hassan Gouled Aptidon, an <b>Issa Somali</b> politician, became its first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1896 France combined Obock and its protectorates over the Sultanate of Tadjoura and the <b>Issa Somali</b> coast into the colony of the Cote francaise des Somalis, with its capital at the new port of Djibouti, founded by Leonce Lagarde.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-somaliland">French Somaliland</a></li><li><blockquote>After a referendum in March 1967, in which a majority voted to remain French amid protests and allegations of fraud, French Somaliland was renamed the French Territory of the Afars and the <b>Issas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas">French Territory of the Afars and the Issas</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1884 Leonce Lagarde became commandant and expanded French influence by treaties with the Sultanate of Tadjoura and <b>Issa Somali</b> clans.</blockquote><a href="/state/obock">Obock</a></li></ul></section>
